.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt CHDIR 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm chdir ,
.Nm fchdir ,
.Nm getcwd
.Nd change or obtain the current working directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn chdir "const char *path"
.Ft int
.Fn fchdir "int fd"
.Ft char *
.Fn getcwd "char *buf" "size_t size"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn chdir
changes the calling process's working directory, which its threads
share, to
.Fa path .
A relative path is resolved from the current working directory.
The target must be a directory.
Directory search uses the caller's effective user and group IDs and
supplementary groups; the target also requires search permission.
The KRFFS root is treated as a directory with mode 0755.
.Pp
The kernel records an absolute path of at most 4095 bytes for the new
working directory: in the record the threads share when it is shorter
than 64 bytes, and in kernel memory when it is longer.
On KRFFS it reconstructs that path from the directory's parents.
On another file system it uses the resolved directory's path operation
when it has one, the reconstruction succeeds and the mount's prefix plus
the path fits 4096 bytes with its terminator; otherwise it stores the
request with dot components collapsed after resolution.
A relative path joined to a working directory must also fit 4096 bytes;
Linux bounds only the path the caller passes.
The working directory may also be a synthetic directory under
.Pa /dev
or
.Pa /proc .
A process directory reached through
.Pa /proc/self
is recorded with the actual process ID.
.Pp
.Fn fchdir
makes the directory
.Fa fd
is open on the working directory.
Its path is the one the descriptor's
.Li *at
calls resolve against: a directory of a file system that rebuilds its
path is named where it is now, after a rename too.
On KRFFS, FAT32 and exFAT a directory a descriptor refers to is not
removed, as
.Xr rmdir 2
says.
ext2 and tmpfs remove it, and
.Fn fchdir
of a directory removed since returns
.Er ENOENT ,
where Linux enters the removed directory.
A descriptor on anything but a directory returns
.Er ENOTDIR .
Entering takes search permission on the directory, as
.Fn chdir
does, and a directory opened with
.Dv O_PATH
serves as well as one opened for reading, as on Linux.
A directory outside the root
.Xr chroot 2
gave the process returns
.Er ENOENT ,
where Linux enters it.
.Pp
A working directory holds a reference to its mount, which keeps the
mount from being unmounted, and to its node on a file system that counts
them.
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
take no reference.
KRFFS, the boot root and a mounted volume, counts no reference to a node;
the working directory keeps the mount and the node all the same.
.Xr rmdir 2
refuses a directory that is a process's working directory with
.Er EBUSY
on KRFFS, FAT32 and exFAT.
ext2 and tmpfs remove it, as Linux does, and leave the working directory
on the removed directory, with its path kept.
On a mounted KRFFS volume
.Fn chdir
keeps the directory from removal, by an open record as an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor keeps one, from the lookup that finds it until the working
directory records it, since the checks between may wait for the disk.
Changing the working directory releases the previous reference.
.Pp
.Fn getcwd
copies the stored absolute path, including its terminating null byte,
to
.Fa buf .
The path is the one from the process's root directory, which
.Xr chroot 2
may have moved.
.Fa size
is the buffer capacity in bytes and must include room for that byte.
With a non-null buffer, size zero gives
.Er EINVAL
and an insufficient size gives
.Er ERANGE .
When
.Fa buf
is null, musl obtains the path in a temporary buffer and returns an
allocated copy; the caller releases it with
.Fn free .
.Pp
The kernel calls are 12 for
.Fn chdir ,
133 for
.Fn fchdir
and 183 for
.Fn getcwd .
The raw
.Fn getcwd
call requires a non-null buffer and returns the length including the
terminating null byte.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn chdir
and
.Fn fchdir
return 0 on success, or -1 and set
.Va errno
on failure.
.Fn getcwd
returns the buffer pointer on success, or a null pointer and sets
.Va errno
on failure.
The raw kernel calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/dirprobe.c
creates
.Pa /work ,
changes to it, and checks that
.Fn getcwd
reports
.Pa /work .
It creates a file by a relative name, then changes to
.Pa ..
and checks that the working directory is
.Pa / .
.Pa tests/musl/atcallmusl.c
changes to a directory through a descriptor on it, and checks that
.Fn fchdir
on a file returns
.Er ENOTDIR .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENAMETOOLONG
.It Er EACCES
Search permission is denied on a directory in the path or on the target.
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not open.
.It Er EBUSY
Path resolution enters a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Er EFAULT
The path cannot be read or the output buffer cannot be written.
The raw
.Fn getcwd
call also rejects a null buffer.
.It Er EINVAL
The output buffer size is zero.
.It Er EIO
Path lookup or metadata access fails, including access to a removed device.
.It Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
The supplied path or its joined absolute form exceeds 4095 bytes, a
component exceeds 255 bytes, or symbolic-link expansion exceeds 4095
bytes; a reconstructed path too long to store falls back to the
collapsed request instead.
.It Er ENOENT
The path is empty or a component or synthetic process directory is missing.
A symbolic link has an empty target.
The directory
.Fn fchdir
names is outside the process's root, or ext2 or tmpfs removed it.
.It Er ENOMEM
Path lookup cannot allocate a file-system node, a mounted KRFFS volume
cannot allocate the open record that keeps the directory during the
call, the kernel has no page to copy the path into or no memory for a
working directory path of 64 bytes or more, or musl cannot allocate the
requested copy of the working directory.
.It Er ENOTDIR
The target or a component used as a directory is not a directory, or
.Fa fd
is not open on a directory.
.It Er ERANGE
The output buffer is too small for the path and terminating null byte.
.It Er ESRCH
The raw
.Fn getcwd
call has no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr access 2 ,
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr open 2
